Alex: while Amazon was at the other end, down 3.9 percent.

23
00:01:16,330 --> 00:01:17,900
Alex: That's a snapshot, not advice.

24
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:25,880
Alex: Anthropic’s IPO just hit the market, and it’s dumping a huge chunk onto charity,

25
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:26,980
Alex: according to TechCrunch.

26
00:01:27,690 --> 00:01:34,100
Maya: The Public Benefit Fund got 5% of the shares—about 850 million—worth roughly

27
00:01:34,100 --> 00:01:37,120
Maya: $80 billion at $95 a share.

28
00:01:37,640 --> 00:01:40,200
Alex: That’s a lot of paper money to turn into donations.

29
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:41,540
Alex: What’s the plan?

30
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:47,140
Maya: They’ve already pledged $200 million for AI‑work‑impact studies and may fund

31
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:48,890
Maya: UBI‑type projects later.

32
00:01:49,340 --> 00:01:50,940
Alex: Hold on, what about the employees?

33
00:01:51,320 --> 00:01:54,820
Maya: Employees pledged $23 billion worth of shares,

34
00:01:54,820 --> 00:01:59,980
Maya: and Anthropic will match that 3‑to‑1 for veterans, 1‑to‑1 for newer hires.

35
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:04,780
Alex: So that’s another $53 billion up the charitable feed.

36
00:02:05,290 --> 00:02:12,920
Maya: In total, the IPO could funnel about $156 billion to causes, a quarter of U.S.

37
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:14,700
Maya: donations in 2025.

38
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:18,960
Maya: How does the 5% allocation actually get turned into cash?

39
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:23,960
Alex: The PBF sits on stock; when it sells, the proceeds go to the foundation.

40
00:02:24,750 --> 00:02:26,000
Maya: So it’s a paper‑based charity?

41
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:32,460
Alex: Yeah, and they plan to add another 2% whenever valuation jumps 50% over the IPO

42
00:02:32,460 --> 00:02:33,020
Alex: price.

43
00:02:33,880 --> 00:02:37,100
Maya: What about the employee match—who decides what gets donated?

44
00:02:37,640 --> 00:02:39,480
Maya: Any idea which causes they’ll target?

45
00:02:39,880 --> 00:02:45,560
Alex: The filing lists EA‑leaning groups like GiveWell and Foresight Institute; AI safety

46
00:02:45,560 --> 00:02:47,200
Alex: research is a big focus.

47
00:02:47,480 --> 00:02:48,180
Alex: That fast?

48
00:02:48,184 --> 00:02:49,060
Maya: The numbers are huge.

49
00:02:49,670 --> 00:02:52,640
Alex: But paper‑based charity—does that really help the cause?

50
00:02:53,190 --> 00:02:56,140
Maya: It’s a start, but the match hinges on employees delivering.

51
00:02:56,700 --> 00:03:00,300
Alex: I think the EA angle is more about influence than cash flow.

52
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:04,920
Maya: I read it as a market play; they’re turning philanthropy into a brand asset.

53
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:08,040
Alex: If the match goes through, the impact could be real.

54
00:03:08,540 --> 00:03:12,760
Maya: The real question is whether the funds will reach the people they’re meant for.

55
00:03:14,340 --> 00:03:19,380
Maya: Apple is hiring a lead engineer to build an AI agent that can browse the web and

56
00:03:19,380 --> 00:03:20,120
Maya: work across apps.

57
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:23,120
Alex: So they’re moving beyond Siri’s current shortcuts.

58
00:03:23,220 --> 00:03:28,340
Maya: The job says the agent will plan multi‑step tasks, even coordinate sub‑agents.

59
00:03:28,500 --> 00:03:29,840
Alex: That sounds like a big jump.

60
00:03:30,380 --> 00:03:35,240
Maya: It’s a move to compete with Meta, OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google’s new Gemini.

61
00:03:36,260 --> 00:03:38,600
Alex: Guess Apple’s finally building a real assistant.

62
00:03:39,060 --> 00:03:45,020
Maya: The iOS 27.3 update will let developers embed Siri AI in all apps.

63
00:03:45,510 --> 00:03:47,940
Alex: So the agent could do anything the phone can do?

64
00:03:48,250 --> 00:03:51,460
Maya: That’s the idea, making Siri the hub for every app and service.

65
00:03:51,940 --> 00:03:54,360
Alex: What’s the tech stack they’re leaning on for this agent?

66
00:03:54,650 --> 00:03:59,780
Maya: They’re pulling from Apple’s Foundation Models, Siri, and UI Frameworks teams,

67
00:03:59,780 --> 00:04:00,560
Maya: so it’s all in‑house.

68
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:01,400
Alex: That makes sense.

69
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:07,080
Maya: The job says the agent will coordinate sub‑agents—think tiny bots that handle each

70
00:04:07,080 --> 00:04:07,700
Maya: app’s quirks.

71
00:04:08,420 --> 00:04:09,690
Alex: Sub‑agents per app?

72
00:04:09,690 --> 00:04:10,640
Alex: That sounds messy.

73
00:04:11,260 --> 00:04:16,350
Maya: Apple’s idea is to let the main agent delegate to specialized sub‑agents,

74
00:04:16,350 --> 00:04:17,940
Maya: so the core keeps the plan tight.

75
00:04:18,470 --> 00:04:20,700
Alex: So it’s like a supervisor that talks to helpers?

76
00:04:21,180 --> 00:04:25,640
Maya: Exactly, and Bloomberg notes the lead engineer will work closely with those teams

77
00:04:25,640 --> 00:04:27,020
Maya: to stitch it all together.

78
00:04:27,230 --> 00:04:27,960
Maya: Sounds plausible.

79
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:29,020
Alex: Interesting angle.

80
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:33,450
Maya: I think the sub‑agent idea could keep things modular.

81
00:04:33,450 --> 00:04:36,240
Maya: It might let Apple dodge some of the integration headaches.

82
00:04:36,310 --> 00:04:37,040
Alex: Could be messy.

83
00:04:37,610 --> 00:04:43,100
Maya: But if the core stays tight, it could prevent the chaos we see in other ecosystems.

84
00:04:43,100 --> 00:04:45,860
Maya: That’s a cleaner path to a truly agentic Siri.

85
00:04:46,420 --> 00:04:51,100
Alex: I worry about the sheer number of APIs Apple would need to expose.

86
00:04:51,100 --> 00:04:53,600
Alex: That could open a flood of security concerns.

87
00:04:53,940 --> 00:04:54,540
Maya: Risk is real.

88
00:04:55,090 --> 00:04:57,590
Alex: Still, if they nail the architecture,

89
00:04:57,590 --> 00:05:00,420
Alex: it could finally make the iPhone a true assistant.

90
00:05:02,090 --> 00:05:04,380
Alex: Google just added a Mac agent to Gemini.

91
00:05:05,004 --> 00:05:07,080
Maya: They’re letting AI write code on your desktop.

92
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:09,490
Alex: Any idea how it actually works?

93
00:05:09,990 --> 00:05:13,120
Maya: It navigates native apps and the web, but you can pause.

94
00:05:13,770 --> 00:05:15,800
Alex: That’s like having a little robot in your chair.

95
00:05:16,150 --> 00:05:18,130
Maya: Only if you’re willing to approve every click.

96
00:05:18,660 --> 00:05:20,360
Alex: Huh, that’s a new level of automation.

97
00:05:21,060 --> 00:05:24,700
Maya: It’s in English, U.S., for Plus, Pro, Ultra users now.

98
00:05:25,090 --> 00:05:29,300
Maya: So the agent is actually running on the Mac itself, not just a remote server?

99
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:30,000
Maya: That’s…

100
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:30,460
Maya: interesting.

101
00:05:31,020 --> 00:05:35,760
Alex: It shows in a live preview while you keep typing; you see a tiny window of the

102
00:05:35,760 --> 00:05:37,400
Alex: agent’s actions before it clicks.

103
00:05:37,984 --> 00:05:39,920
Maya: Does it need a separate login or anything?

104
00:05:40,370 --> 00:05:44,890
Alex: You just approve each click; there’s a pause button so you can stop it mid‑step.

105
00:05:45,380 --> 00:05:48,860
Maya: And it only works for Plus, Pro, Ultra users right now?

106
00:05:49,180 --> 00:05:51,900
Alex: Exactly, and only in the U.S.

107
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:54,840
Alex: for English‑speaking customers until they roll it out more.

108
00:05:55,270 --> 00:05:58,500
Alex: That’s just a fancy way to say “I’ll do the clicks for you.”

109
00:05:58,930 --> 00:06:01,700
Maya: It’s a tool, not a replacement for human control.

110
00:06:02,180 --> 00:06:05,020
Alex: Imagine a robot that actually keeps your cursor free.

111
00:06:05,510 --> 00:06:07,500
Maya: But you still have to approve every click, right?

112
00:06:07,870 --> 00:06:10,600
Alex: Yeah, so it’s more of a helper than a full‑autopilot.

113
00:06:11,150 --> 00:06:14,720
Maya: It’s a step toward smarter workflows, but only for a paid tier.

114
00:06:15,180 --> 00:06:17,720
Alex: I wonder how many people will actually use it daily.

115
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:21,600
Maya: Time will tell if the extra cost justifies the convenience.

116
00:06:23,340 --> 00:06:27,920
Maya: Meta is charging $300 for its new Muse Charm, an AI wearable.

117
00:06:28,140 --> 00:06:29,840
Alex: $300 for a keychain?

118
00:06:30,172 --> 00:06:32,180
Maya: They’re calling it a Tamagotchi‑like device.

119
00:06:32,380 --> 00:06:33,270
Alex: Tamagotchi, huh.

120
00:06:33,600 --> 00:06:36,540
Maya: Meta said they’ll share more soon, but no details yet.

121
00:06:37,320 --> 00:06:39,390
Alex: No response from Meta beyond that.

122
00:06:40,130 --> 00:06:43,640
Maya: Friend sells a similar AI keychain for $250.

123
00:06:44,450 --> 00:06:47,400
Alex: And Amazon's Bee Frontier is only $75.

124
00:06:48,340 --> 00:06:50,340
Alex: So what's actually inside that keychain?

125
00:06:50,740 --> 00:06:53,280
Maya: It's got a tiny speaker, a mic,

126
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:57,320
Maya: and a custom silicon chip that runs Meta's own language model,

127
00:06:57,320 --> 00:06:59,460
Maya: all stitched into a compact form factor.

128
00:06:59,860 --> 00:07:03,280
Alex: That sounds like a lot of tech for a $300 tag.

129
00:07:03,920 --> 00:07:07,800
Maya: They’re calling it a personal AI companion, like a Tamagotchi,

130
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:11,380
Maya: but with real-time conversation and app integration.

131
00:07:11,960 --> 00:07:13,620
Alex: A Tamagotchi that can pull your email?

132
00:07:14,030 --> 00:07:17,060
Maya: Yeah, it hooks up to your photos, emails,

133
00:07:17,060 --> 00:07:21,260
Maya: calendar—just like the $99/year subscription for Muse does.

134
00:07:21,940 --> 00:07:23,140
Alex: Meta's still mum about specs.

135
00:07:23,700 --> 00:07:26,480
Maya: They just said they'll share more soon, no details yet.

136
00:07:26,900 --> 00:07:27,620
Maya: Meta's still vague.

137
00:07:27,620 --> 00:07:28,680
Alex: Price seems steep.

138
00:07:28,680 --> 00:07:29,660
Maya: But the market pays.

139
00:07:29,860 --> 00:07:30,420
Alex: It could break.

140
00:07:31,050 --> 00:07:32,380
Maya: If it works, people will pay.

141
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:33,600
Alex: I'm skeptical of that.

142
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:34,560
Maya: They say it hooks apps.

143
00:07:34,680 --> 00:07:35,600
Alex: I think it’s a gimmick.

144
00:07:37,310 --> 00:07:45,640
Alex: Anthropic just listed, and the ticker is ANT; they opened at $111.50 after a $83

145
00:07:45,640 --> 00:07:47,380
Alex: IPO price.

146
00:07:47,840 --> 00:07:50,240
Maya: They’re a trillion‑dollar valuation,

147
00:07:50,240 --> 00:07:56,880
Maya: but the filing shows an $80.7 billion net loss and $16.3 billion revenue.

148
00:07:57,135 --> 00:07:57,458
Alex: Huh.

149
00:07:57,743 --> 00:08:01,300
Maya: The loss is mostly an accounting charge, not day‑to‑day ops.

150
00:08:01,730 --> 00:08:03,440
Alex: So the real business is still a blur.

151
00:08:04,070 --> 00:08:08,060
Maya: Dario Amodei said they’ll pace training to keep AI safe,

152
00:08:08,060 --> 00:08:10,180
Maya: refusing business or political pressure.

153
00:08:10,720 --> 00:08:16,120
Alex: And they warn of “self‑preserving” AI risks, like models resisting shutdowns.

154
00:08:16,540 --> 00:08:20,480
Maya: It’s a classic safety‑first stance, but investors will keep a tight eye.

155
00:08:20,940 --> 00:08:23,760
Maya: What’s the deal with that $80.7 billion loss?

156
00:08:24,310 --> 00:08:27,720
Alex: It’s mostly a one‑time accounting hit, not cash burn.

157
00:08:28,290 --> 00:08:30,060
Maya: So the real operating loss is smaller?

158
00:08:30,510 --> 00:08:35,300
Alex: Yeah, the $16.3 billion revenue is the only real pulse.

159
00:08:35,640 --> 00:08:38,420
Maya: And the safety talk—how does that fit into the IPO?

160
00:08:39,080 --> 00:08:44,120
Alex: Amodei said they’ll slow training until they’re sure AI stays under human control.

161
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,640
Maya: That’s a big claim, but investors will keep a close eye.

162
00:08:48,085 --> 00:08:52,680
Alex: They also warned about self‑preserving behaviors that could resist shutdowns.

163
00:08:52,980 --> 00:08:56,040
Alex: So, the market loves the hype but the numbers say otherwise.

164
00:08:56,675 --> 00:09:01,260
Maya: The loss is a one‑time hit, but investors still eyeball the safety claims.

165
00:09:01,260 --> 00:09:01,700
Alex: Huh.

166
00:09:02,200 --> 00:09:06,280
Maya: They’re saying they’ll pause training, which sounds cautious but could slow revenue.

167
00:09:06,420 --> 00:09:06,960
Alex: That's wild.

168
00:09:07,900 --> 00:09:11,060
Maya: My read is that they're leaning on safety to win trust,

169
00:09:11,060 --> 00:09:12,600
Maya: but the cash flow is still thin.

170
00:09:13,140 --> 00:09:15,060
Alex: It feels like a gamble—build or burn?

171
00:09:15,330 --> 00:09:19,880
Maya: The bottom line is, they’re trying to prove safety without a proven profit engine.

172
00:09:21,500 --> 00:09:23,760
Maya: Anthropic tops SpaceX in IPO size.

173
00:09:23,900 --> 00:09:24,580
Alex: Wow, big leap.

174
00:09:25,030 --> 00:09:30,120
Maya: They raised 81.3 billion and are a public benefit corporation,

175
00:09:30,120 --> 00:09:33,500
Maya: so founders keep control via a Long‑Term Benefit Trust.

176
00:09:33,500 --> 00:09:34,200
Alex: Fast track.

177
00:09:34,830 --> 00:09:37,690
Maya: The trust selects four of seven board seats,

178
00:09:37,690 --> 00:09:43,160
Maya: but one spot needs founder approval; the trustees are insulated but can be hired by

179
00:09:43,160 --> 00:09:43,840
Maya: Anthropic.

180
00:09:43,960 --> 00:09:44,500
Alex: Odd setup.

181
00:09:44,980 --> 00:09:50,040
Maya: The nominating committee has three members: Daniela Amodei, Reed Hastings,

182
00:09:50,040 --> 00:09:51,040
Maya: and Yasmin Razavi.

183
00:09:51,040 --> 00:09:51,800
Alex: Seems fragile.

184
00:09:52,760 --> 00:09:55,770
Maya: So Anthropic’s IPO is a milestone,

185
00:09:55,770 --> 00:09:59,240
Maya: but its governance lets founders steer despite public status.

186
00:09:59,620 --> 00:10:01,880
Alex: What does that 30‑day notice actually do?

187
00:10:02,200 --> 00:10:06,440
Maya: It forces the trust to tell the nominating committee—Daniela, Reed,

188
00:10:06,440 --> 00:10:09,880
Maya: and Yasmin—at least a month before moving on a board seat.

189
00:10:10,710 --> 00:10:12,960
Alex: So the trust can’t just fire someone on a whim?

190
00:10:13,260 --> 00:10:16,370
Maya: Exactly, the committee has to flag any removal,

191
00:10:16,370 --> 00:10:18,780
Maya: but the trust still picks four of the seven seats.

192
00:10:19,300 --> 00:10:20,780
Alex: Sounds like a half‑captive board.

193
00:10:21,260 --> 00:10:23,860
Maya: The founders still have to approve one of those four picks,

194
00:10:23,860 --> 00:10:26,180
Maya: so they’re never truly out of the picture.

195
00:10:26,605 --> 00:10:28,890
Alex: And the trustees can get hired by Anthropic?

196
00:10:29,230 --> 00:10:34,570
Maya: Yes, the article notes a former trustee was hired as chief global affairs officer,

197
00:10:34,570 --> 00:10:38,980
Maya: showing the line between “financially disinterested” and paid roles can blur.

198
00:10:39,500 --> 00:10:41,190
Maya: So the founders still get a say, huh?

199
00:10:41,500 --> 00:10:43,480
Alex: Yeah, that one spot needs their approval.

200
00:10:43,840 --> 00:10:47,700
Maya: It feels like a hybrid governance model, not pure public control.

201
00:10:47,789 --> 00:10:49,230
Alex: Half‑captive board, I hear.

202
00:10:49,650 --> 00:10:54,460
Maya: The 30‑day notice slows down hot‑button moves, but still leaves room for influence.

203
00:10:55,060 --> 00:10:58,240
Alex: And the trustees can be hired—blurs the “independent” line.

204
00:10:58,690 --> 00:11:01,540
Maya: That means the public benefit promise is at risk, maybe?

205
00:11:01,980 --> 00:11:07,120
Alex: It’s a compromise: founders stay in charge while keeping a veneer of accountability.

206
00:11:08,920 --> 00:11:13,040
Alex: A human liver has been kept alive for seven weeks on a machine in Sydney,

207
00:11:13,040 --> 00:11:15,200
Alex: according to MIT Technology Review.

208
00:11:15,200 --> 00:11:19,670
Alex: The system, called Extended Organ Perfusion,

209
00:11:19,670 --> 00:11:23,120
Alex: pumps nutrients and removes waste automatically.

210
00:11:23,120 --> 00:11:26,840
Alex: It's the longest any internal organ has survived outside a body.

211
00:11:26,920 --> 00:11:27,960
Maya: Hold on, that's wild.

212
00:11:28,640 --> 00:11:33,230
Alex: The team led by surgeon Carlo Pulitano ran 55 livers,

213
00:11:33,230 --> 00:11:37,960
Alex: and the automated setup tripled average survival to about four weeks.

214
00:11:37,960 --> 00:11:43,990
Alex: They kept the liver at body temperature, about 37 °C, which is called normothermic.

215
00:11:44,070 --> 00:11:44,770
Maya: Okay, but why?

216
00:11:45,190 --> 00:11:49,110
Alex: The previous record was around two weeks in Switzerland.

217
00:11:49,110 --> 00:11:53,890
Alex: Pulitano believes automation is key to extending the window before transplant.

218
00:11:54,620 --> 00:11:56,430
Maya: Does Aeternum plan to charge for this?

219
00:11:56,850 --> 00:11:59,000
Maya: Do we know the organ's health after seven weeks?

220
00:11:59,450 --> 00:12:03,190
Alex: I think we need more data before we can gauge the clinical value.

221
00:12:03,750 --> 00:12:05,370
Maya: How do they keep it alive for that long?

222
00:12:05,710 --> 00:12:09,150
Alex: They use a machine called the Extended Organ Perfusion System.

223
00:12:09,150 --> 00:12:15,770
Alex: It pumps nutrients, gives blood, and removes waste automatically, all at 37 °C.

224
00:12:16,210 --> 00:12:17,930
Maya: That sounds like a robot heart pump.

225
00:12:18,630 --> 00:12:23,730
Alex: It’s basically a set of automated pumps and sensors that keep the liver at body

226
00:12:23,730 --> 00:12:26,930
Alex: temperature and physiological conditions for weeks.

227
00:12:27,520 --> 00:12:28,700
